WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.820
Kids that did well in school
did well in French, but nobody

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:04.290
really learned to speak French.

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:07.320
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here,
and, and today I want to talk

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:12.870
about what I would do, how I would
teach language in a classroom.

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:17.160
Uh, remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:18.570
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:21.930
And, uh, if you follow me on
a podcast service, uh, you

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:22.770
know, please leave a comment.

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:23.640
I do appreciate it.

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:29.955
So I'm gonna talk about teaching
language, a language in a classroom,

00:00:30.135 --> 00:00:34.275
which is something that I have
essentially never done, but I'm gonna

00:00:34.275 --> 00:00:37.065
begin by telling two little stories.

00:00:37.755 --> 00:00:42.045
Uh, first of all, I remember very clearly
that we had a teacher in high school who

00:00:42.045 --> 00:00:46.575
was originally from the UK and this was in
Montreal and, uh, we were learning French.

00:00:46.575 --> 00:00:47.655
He was the French teacher.

00:00:48.105 --> 00:00:52.004
And I can remember that when we would
try to speak French in class, sometimes

00:00:52.004 --> 00:00:56.225
he would preface that by saying "all
right, you guys, now we're gonna talk

00:00:56.225 --> 00:01:01.425
frog language", which is maybe funny, and
people chuckled a bit but it really didn't

00:01:01.425 --> 00:01:06.825
inspire much enthusiasm for what we were
about to attempt to do, speak French.

00:01:07.455 --> 00:01:13.275
And then the second thing I remember was
when I was, uh, a student in France, I had

00:01:13.275 --> 00:01:18.985
a job I used to go twice I think a week
I would go to a French family for lunch

00:01:19.465 --> 00:01:23.545
and I was paid and I was given a small
amount and I was given a, a wonderful

00:01:23.545 --> 00:01:26.785
meal, much better than the student
restaurant fair that I was used to.

00:01:27.205 --> 00:01:30.505
And my job was to speak English
to the kids and the family.

00:01:30.685 --> 00:01:33.055
There were, the parents were
there and they had two kids.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:35.215
They all come home for
lunch and I was invited.

00:01:36.145 --> 00:01:39.985
And so I'd finished my lunch
with, uh, a couple of glasses

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:41.215
of wine that they would give me.

00:01:41.635 --> 00:01:48.054
And then I would bicycle halfway across
Paris to, uh, the Agricultural Institute.

00:01:48.675 --> 00:01:51.655
... I don't know, actually
called whatever it was.

00:01:52.105 --> 00:01:55.165
And I had to run in there and I
had to start this language lab.

00:01:55.225 --> 00:01:59.095
So now we're talking about 1966/65.

00:01:59.520 --> 00:01:59.910
Okay.

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:01.440
That's a long time ago.

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:07.140
And so here are all these aspiring,
you know, French farmers, I

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:11.430
guess, that we're studying at
the ... or whatever it's called.

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:17.490
And, uh, so, and they would be listening
to stuff on their, they had these tape

00:02:17.495 --> 00:02:19.470
recorders, so it was a big language lab.

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:20.730
So there was like a big room.

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:24.305
There were people sitting at their
tape recorder, listening to this stuff.

00:02:24.515 --> 00:02:28.805
I can't remember what my function was
other than to turn it on, or if people had

00:02:28.805 --> 00:02:30.935
questions, but mostly they just sat there.

00:02:31.385 --> 00:02:35.435
And one of the guys at the back of the
class, he took his big earphones off

00:02:35.525 --> 00:02:45.000
he slammed them down on his desk and
he said ... in other words, I've been

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:49.920
studying French or English for 10 years,
and I still don't understand anything.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:53.880
So that again, I remember that quite
vividly, becasue it, it made me laugh.

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:57.720
I remember that they had stuff in the
text that they were listening to terms

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:02.520
that I didn't even know in English like
stubble mulch, stubble mulch tillage.

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:05.160
I remember stubble mulch tillage.

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:08.040
So that's kind of my
experience with the classroom.

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:13.380
And I, I would say that in, um, in
high school or in elementary school,

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:18.660
the French was most uninteresting
and, uh, you know, kids that did well

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:22.110
in school did well in French, but
nobody really learned to speak French.

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:25.410
Uh, when I got to university I've
mentioned before I had this Dr.

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:30.360
Maurice Rabotan who was a great professor
who got me motivated to learn French

00:03:30.365 --> 00:03:32.070
really inspired me to learn French.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:34.000
And then my French took off.

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:39.720
So if I were a teacher, uh, if
I've envisioned the classroom.

00:03:40.109 --> 00:03:43.079
So you can have a situation where
the teacher is a native speaker.

00:03:43.769 --> 00:03:48.089
You can have a situation where the teacher
is not a native speaker but speaks the

00:03:48.095 --> 00:03:50.220
language that he or she is teaching.

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:55.230
Well, you can have a situation where
the teacher doesn't speak that language

00:03:55.234 --> 00:03:59.459
well, and you could even have a situation
where the teacher doesn't speak that

00:03:59.464 --> 00:04:05.279
language at all today in the modern world
of the internet and MP3 and so forth.

00:04:05.279 --> 00:04:12.450
And so I don't think it really matters
whether the teacher, uh, is fluent speaker

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:17.250
of the language or not better if he or
she is, I think better if he or she is a

00:04:17.255 --> 00:04:22.230
native speaker, because I think that is,
is more inspirational for the learner that

00:04:22.235 --> 00:04:26.640
they're learning the language from the
person who has that culture, that language

00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:28.740
in them, but it's not a condition.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:35.715
And there are so many opportunities
for the learner to hear the language,

00:04:35.805 --> 00:04:38.505
first of all, obviously, to read
anything they wanna read in the

00:04:38.505 --> 00:04:44.655
language and also to hear the language
via MP3 files, to discover content of

00:04:44.655 --> 00:04:53.355
interest, even to connect with tutors
and rooms could be organized not like

00:04:53.355 --> 00:04:59.055
that language lab, uh, situation in
France in 1965, but where you people

00:04:59.055 --> 00:05:02.655
could have direct contact with tutors
in the language that they're learning.

00:05:02.655 --> 00:05:04.785
So the teacher needn't provide that.

00:05:04.815 --> 00:05:10.605
The language the teacher to my mind
is first and foremost is a motivator

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:16.170
like Maurice Rabotan motivated me
to learn French with tremendous

00:05:16.170 --> 00:05:19.440
consequences because I went to
France, I became fluent in French.

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:22.560
I acquired that confidence
that I can learn languages.

00:05:22.710 --> 00:05:25.740
So he had a tremendous impact on my life.

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:29.924
But a teacher, you know,
obviously because Mr.

00:05:29.924 --> 00:05:33.614
Rabotan was French that was part of
what inspired me, but I think a very

00:05:33.614 --> 00:05:38.955
good teacher, very encouraging teacher
can stimulate students and inspire them

00:05:38.955 --> 00:05:42.854
and help them, even if the language
that the student is trying to learn is

00:05:42.854 --> 00:05:44.294
not the native language of the teacher.

00:05:44.565 --> 00:05:46.994
I think we need to look
at the language classroom.

00:05:46.994 --> 00:05:52.215
Like if, if it were me, I would
encourage, I would encourage my students

00:05:52.215 --> 00:05:56.534
to listen and read, obviously, uh, I
would encourage them to use uh, if I

00:05:56.534 --> 00:06:01.635
were a teacher in a classroom, I could
have 25 kids and I could set aside, you

00:06:01.635 --> 00:06:06.465
know, a range of content, 20 stories
that I know are suitable for them from

00:06:06.465 --> 00:06:10.125
a number of different perspectives and
they can choose whichever one they want.

00:06:10.485 --> 00:06:14.355
And I would be able to follow
how active they are, how much

00:06:14.355 --> 00:06:17.265
they're reading, how much they're
listening, how many LingQs they're

00:06:17.265 --> 00:06:19.455
creating, how much they're writing.

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:23.940
Uh, I could organize for them to talk
to look if I'm a native speaker, fine.

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:28.290
If I'm not, or if I'm not very fluent
in that language, I can arrange for them

00:06:28.410 --> 00:06:32.460
twice a week to have online contacts
or maybe small group of them to have

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:37.470
online contacts with, uh, a teacher,
a native speaker from the country.

00:06:37.995 --> 00:06:41.205
Uh, and they could talk about
things, uh, about the country or

00:06:41.205 --> 00:06:44.865
about whatever the students are,
uh, reading and listening to.

00:06:44.955 --> 00:06:47.925
And I wouldn't have to
create content all the time.

00:06:47.925 --> 00:06:49.245
I wouldn't have to create lessons.

00:06:49.335 --> 00:06:53.115
I wouldn't have to prepare like
a lot of teachers spend a lot

00:06:53.115 --> 00:06:54.585
of time preparing their lessons.

00:06:54.975 --> 00:06:57.405
Uh, I could follow exactly
what they're doing.

00:06:57.975 --> 00:06:59.865
Also I wouldn't test them.

00:07:00.255 --> 00:07:05.070
I would rely on keeping track of how
active they are because I, as I've said

00:07:05.070 --> 00:07:09.390
before, many times, what matters is
how active are they with the language.

00:07:09.659 --> 00:07:11.549
Some are gonna do better than others.

00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:15.645
I don't want to discourage the ones
that are, you know, don't pronounce

00:07:15.645 --> 00:07:20.145
as well or don't they don't seem to
do as well for whatever reason they

00:07:20.145 --> 00:07:22.695
might start doing better later on.

00:07:22.700 --> 00:07:26.385
So if they're struggling at an early
stage, why would I discourage them?

00:07:26.625 --> 00:07:30.375
Rather I would want to control,
I'd wanna see how active they are

00:07:30.435 --> 00:07:33.585
and if they're not very active,
how do I make them more active?

00:07:33.735 --> 00:07:36.215
Is it because the content
is of no interest to them?

00:07:36.515 --> 00:07:41.225
Uh, I'd have to try to find out
with each of these students, if

00:07:41.225 --> 00:07:42.815
possible is not always possible.

00:07:43.055 --> 00:07:45.605
How do I motivate them
to become more active?

00:07:45.725 --> 00:07:48.425
The goal is to make them more active.

00:07:48.905 --> 00:07:52.495
And so once you accept that the
teacher doesn't have to bring

00:07:52.805 --> 00:07:54.095
the language to the student.

00:07:54.365 --> 00:07:56.135
So the teacher doesn't have to be native.

00:07:56.135 --> 00:07:58.265
Doesn't even have to be
proficient in the language.

00:07:58.535 --> 00:08:03.310
It now opens up the possibility
that many more schools can offer

00:08:03.310 --> 00:08:07.300
languages which they now don't
offer because they don't have an

00:08:07.300 --> 00:08:09.940
accredited teacher in that language.

00:08:10.360 --> 00:08:15.690
So you could offer five languages in
the classroom and encourage them to

00:08:15.690 --> 00:08:20.010
go off and study, or even sit in the
classroom and listen and read right.

00:08:20.010 --> 00:08:20.849
They're in the classroom.

00:08:21.239 --> 00:08:25.770
Uh, if the classroom is only in
one language, of course we can have

00:08:25.950 --> 00:08:30.359
conversations in the classroom and the
kids can talk about what they have been

00:08:30.359 --> 00:08:35.075
reading and, uh, they can even talk
about words that they've been saving,

00:08:35.075 --> 00:08:39.365
that they don't fully understand, and
other kids can comment on what they

00:08:39.365 --> 00:08:41.284
think the word means and how it's used.

00:08:41.284 --> 00:08:43.534
And we can have examples
of how that word is used.

00:08:43.685 --> 00:08:46.745
If everyone is studying the
same language, it's much easier.

00:08:47.255 --> 00:08:52.630
Uh, however, If in fact, some
people in that classroom, you

00:08:52.630 --> 00:08:54.190
know, don't wanna study French.

00:08:54.190 --> 00:08:57.970
And I've said before in Canada, you
know, it's like your civic obligation

00:08:58.210 --> 00:09:00.670
to, to patriotic obligation.

00:09:00.670 --> 00:09:03.430
If you're an English speaker, you
gotta learn French, but maybe the

00:09:03.430 --> 00:09:06.160
kid doesn't wanna learn French, but
maybe he wants to learn Spanish or

00:09:06.165 --> 00:09:08.020
he wants to learn Chinese or Russian.

00:09:08.415 --> 00:09:14.565
So then if we can set it up so that
maybe out of 25 kids 15 are gonna do

00:09:14.565 --> 00:09:19.185
French, but another 10 are gonna study
other languages of interest to them.

00:09:19.185 --> 00:09:21.495
And we set it up so that they can do that.

00:09:21.975 --> 00:09:24.465
And then of course, we have to
come up with activities in the

00:09:24.470 --> 00:09:27.015
classroom, which at the very least
can be listening and reading.

00:09:27.165 --> 00:09:29.895
The difficulty is if there's so
many different languages, then

00:09:29.895 --> 00:09:33.135
some of these activities would
have to be again, solitary.

00:09:33.490 --> 00:09:37.030
Uh, such as this, um, putting the
sentences back together again,

00:09:37.030 --> 00:09:40.870
that I'm now doing in beta, uh,
at LingQ and we might devise

00:09:40.870 --> 00:09:43.000
other activities for the kids.

00:09:43.270 --> 00:09:46.810
Obviously it's easier if everyone is
studying the same language, not ideal,

00:09:46.810 --> 00:09:49.930
if you have, uh, different languages,
but then we have to compare that to

00:09:49.930 --> 00:09:54.820
the alternative, which is to sort
of force people to learn a language

00:09:54.820 --> 00:09:56.020
that they're not interested in.

00:09:56.940 --> 00:10:00.270
Uh, because motivation is such a, an
important part of language learning

00:10:00.270 --> 00:10:01.560
that it was the case with me.

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:03.810
I think it's the case with most learners.

00:10:03.930 --> 00:10:07.440
And we see the example in the Canadian
English language, school system

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:12.360
where unmotivated kids learning
French in fact, don't learn French.

00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:14.069
So I think we have to strike a balance.

00:10:14.069 --> 00:10:18.270
So it may not be, be ideal, but again,
I'll be the first to admit that these

00:10:18.270 --> 00:10:23.280
ideas may not be practical, but, um,
I throw those out there as ideas.

00:10:23.310 --> 00:10:26.670
If we look at where language
instruction was in the sixties, when

00:10:26.675 --> 00:10:30.300
I was learning French at school, or
even when I was, or in the fifties,

00:10:30.300 --> 00:10:31.439
I was learning French at school.

00:10:31.785 --> 00:10:37.065
Or in the sixties when I was with these,
uh, students learning, you know, at

00:10:37.065 --> 00:10:43.305
the , as you recall, in, in France, so
many things have changed the internet

00:10:43.335 --> 00:10:48.975
MP3 technology and a whole bunch of
other, uh, technologies and resources,

00:10:48.975 --> 00:10:50.505
language, resources, and so forth.

00:10:50.865 --> 00:10:57.780
So maybe the way languages are taught
in schools will continue to evolve.

00:10:57.780 --> 00:11:02.370
And perhaps some of the ideas that
I've mentioned here may have some

00:11:02.375 --> 00:11:04.260
relevance, uh, for the classroom.

00:11:04.710 --> 00:11:09.060
And I will leave you with, uh, two
older videos where I talk about the

00:11:09.060 --> 00:11:14.070
classroom and individual learning uh,
and you'll see that, uh, maybe some

00:11:14.070 --> 00:11:16.110
of my ideas have evolved over time.

00:11:16.290 --> 00:11:17.550
So thank you very much for listening.

00:11:17.670 --> 00:11:18.069
Bye for now.

